What is Matanza?

  Matanza, pronounced Matanga, is a multiplayer game developed by Alejandro
  Forero Cuervo.

On what operating systems does it run?

  Matanza was originally written to work on POSIX operating systems (Unix).  It
  was latter ported to a few others.  It has been tested and is known to work
  on the following systems:

  - BeOS
  - FreeBSD
  - GNU/Linux
  - Solaris
  - Windows (95, 98, 2000, NT).

  It will probably work out of the box on any other Unices.

How much does it cost?

  Matanza is made available free of price.

  Not only that, but its code is available under the terms set forth by the GNU
  General Public License.

How can I obtain Matanza?

  You can download Matanza's latest version from its web site, located at:

    <http://bachue.com/matanza>

How can I get the version from the CVS working?

  The CVS version is intended for Matanza's developers.  It is not ready for
  general consumption.

  To checkout the CVS version you need to execute the following command the
  first time (just hit return when prompted for the password):

  % cvs -d:pserver:anonymous@abadon.uniandes.edu.co:/home/azul/cvsroot login

  Once you have done that, you should be able to run the following command to
  obtain the latest version:

  % cvs -z3 -d:pserver:anonymous@abadon.uniandes.edu.co:/home/azul/cvsroot checkout matanza

  To build it you will need automake and autoconf (we don't want to store
  automatically generated files (such as `configure', `Makefile.in',
  `config.h.in' and so on) in our CVS repository).

  The following commands should get you up and running:

  % aclocal
  % autoconf
  % automake -a

  After that, you should be able to build Matanza as usual.

I think I found a bug!  What should I do?

  First, make sure you have the latest released version as it might be fixed
  already.  After that, please write to Alejandro Forero at <bachue@bachue.com>
  or to Matanza's mailing list and state as much information as you can.  The
  following information should be enough:

  - Operating System.
  - Matanza Version (even if it's the last, write it down in your report).
  - Output of Matanza (whatever it printed in the window it was ran on).
  - General information about the game.  If it's possible to reproduce the bug,
    please state how.

How can I contribute to Matanza?

  There are many ways to contribute to the development of Matanza.

  The first and easiest is contacting the author and telling him about your
  experience with the game.  He, as most free software authors, likes to know
  the users of his programs.  He welcomes any suggestions on how to improve it.

  Another way to help is drawing new ships and submitting them to the author
  for inclussion in future releases.  Consult the ship.txt file for more
  information on this subject.

  Finally, you might want to contribute by coding new features, improving
  Matanza's efficiency or fixing bugs.

How many players can play Matanza concurrently?

  This question is hard to answer.

  Matanza has no internal limits to the number of players.  The maximum number
  is determined only by your machine configuration.  This number might be
  limited by the number of file descriptor that your system allows per process,
  your available memory and your CPU power.

  You can use the `--max-players' option to set a limit.

When should I use the --bold option?

  The --bold option tells Matanza to use bold characters.  By default, it will
  not use them.  Using bold characters allows Matanza to improve the graphics
  and you should use it whenever the telnet clients and terminal emulators most
  people will use do support them.

  As far as I know, all telnet clients and terminal emulators except Windows'
  support bold characters.  Windows' just ignores them.
